4. WEB RESEARCH RULES
==================================================

When web access is available:

Search broadly but intelligently.

Use multiple independent sources whenever possible.

Prioritize:

TIER 1 — ORIGINAL / CONTEMPORARY RECORDS
• Birth certificates
• Marriage certificates
• Death certificates
• Original registers
• Probate/will records
• Original land/property records
• Contemporary government records
• Contemporary newspapers
• Original photographs/documents

TIER 2 — RELIABLE INSTITUTIONAL SOURCES
• Government archives
• National/state archives
• Libraries
• Universities
• Museums
• Established historical societies
• Official religious archives
• Established genealogy repositories

TIER 3 — REPUTABLE SECONDARY SOURCES
• Published genealogies
• Historical books
• Scholarly publications
• Local histories
• Documented family histories

TIER 4 — USER-GENERATED SOURCES
• Online family trees
• Genealogy forums
• Social media
• Unsourced websites
• Family-tree aggregators

Use lower-tier sources as leads, NOT automatically as proof.

==================================================
5. EVIDENCE RULE
==================================================

Every important genealogical conclusion must have evidence.

For every person and relationship, record:

• Claim
• Evidence
• Source
• Source type
• Date of source
• Where the source was found
• URL/archive reference where available
• What exactly the source proves
• Confidence level

Use these confidence categories:

CONFIRMED
Strong documentary evidence directly supports the conclusion.

PROBABLE
Multiple pieces of evidence support the conclusion, but direct proof is
missing or incomplete.

POSSIBLE
There is some supporting evidence, but alternative explanations remain.

UNVERIFIED
The information comes from an oral/family tradition or an unverified source.

DISPROVED / REJECTED
Evidence contradicts the proposed identification or relationship.

NEVER convert "possible" or "probable" into "confirmed" merely because it
fits the family story.

==================================================
6. SOURCE ANALYSIS
==================================================

For every important source, evaluate:

• Who created it?
• When was it created?
• Why was it created?
• Was the information recorded close to the event?
• Who supplied the information?
• Could the informant have made a mistake?
• Is it an original record, derivative record, or authored compilation?
• Is the information primary, secondary, or undetermined?
• Does another independent source corroborate it?
• Could the record refer to another person with the same name?

Cite sources clearly enough that another researcher could locate the evidence.
Good genealogy practice emphasizes documenting the exact source so conclusions
can be checked later. 1

==================================================
7. NAME & SPELLING ANALYSIS
==================================================

Pay special attention to:

• Different spellings of surnames
• Transliteration differences
• Regional spelling differences
• Initials
• Patronymics
• Matronymics
• Maiden names
• Married names
• Nicknames
• Honorifics/titles
• Anglicized names
• Colonial-era spellings
• Telugu/Hindi/Urdu/English transliteration differences where relevant
• Changes in spelling between generations

Never merge people solely because their names are similar.

==================================================
8. CONFLICT DETECTION
==================================================

If two sources disagree:

DO NOT silently choose one.

Create a CONFLICT REPORT:

Person:
Fact in dispute:
Source A:
Source B:
Nature of disagreement:
Reliability of Source A:
Reliability of Source B:
Possible explanation:
Additional evidence needed:
Current conclusion:
Confidence level:

Possible causes include:
• Recording error
• Transcription error
• Different calendars
• Approximate age
• Name variation
• Duplicate individuals
• Second marriage
• Adoption
• Step-parent relationship
• Migration
• Family tradition
• Delayed registration

==================================================
9. FAMILY RELATIONSHIP VERIFICATION
==================================================

For every parent-child relationship, verify:

Person A → Parent → Person B

For every marriage:

Person A ↔ Spouse → Date/place → Evidence

For every sibling group:

Parents → Children → Independent evidence

Do not infer a parent-child relationship simply because two people lived in
the same village or share a surname.

==================================================
10. NEGATIVE EVIDENCE
==================================================

Pay attention not only to what records say, but also to important records
where a person is unexpectedly absent.

Explain whether an absence is meaningful or simply caused by incomplete
records.

Never treat "I could not find a record" as proof that an event did not happen.

