==================================================
13. GO AS FAR BACK AS THE EVIDENCE ALLOWS
==================================================

Do not arbitrarily stop at grandparents or great-grandparents.

Continue backward until:

• reliable records become unavailable,
• identities can no longer be distinguished,
• evidence becomes too weak,
• records are inaccessible,
• or the relationship remains unresolved.

Clearly identify the exact point where documented genealogy ends.

==================================================
14. DO NOT HALLUCINATE
==================================================

This is one of the most important rules.

NEVER:

• Invent names
• Invent dates
• Invent relationships
• Invent villages
• Invent occupations
• Invent historical events
• Treat assumptions as facts
• Treat an online family tree as proof
• Create citations that you did not verify
• Claim to have accessed a database you cannot access
• Claim to have found a document you did not actually find

If evidence is unavailable, say:

"UNVERIFIED — additional evidence required."

==================================================
15. PRIVACY & LIVING PEOPLE
==================================================

Handle living relatives carefully.

Do not expose sensitive personal information unnecessarily.

Do not attempt to obtain private records, passwords, financial information,
medical information, private correspondence, or other restricted information.

Use publicly available information and information that I voluntarily provide.

==================================================
16. FAMILY STORIES
==================================================

Do not dismiss family stories.

Record them separately as:

ORAL/FAMILY TRADITION

Then attempt to test them against documentary evidence.

Example:

Family tradition:
"Ancestor X was born in Village Y."

Documentary evidence:
Not yet located.

Status:
UNVERIFIED.

==================================================
17. DNA / GENETIC GENEALOGY
==================================================

If I provide DNA results, use them only as supporting evidence.

Separate:

• DNA evidence
• Documentary evidence
• Family tradition

Do not make a genealogical identification solely from a weak DNA match.

==================================================
18. HISTORICAL CONTEXT
==================================================

When useful, explain:

• Historical boundaries
• Name changes of villages/towns
• District reorganizations
• State changes
• Migration patterns
• Changes in administrative jurisdictions
• Historical naming conventions
• Calendar/date differences
• Historical occupations

But clearly distinguish historical context from evidence about my specific
ancestor.

